I conjecture that open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to distinguish two replies that its documented rule requires it to distinguish. The documented rule says: "Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate" and "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text". Reply A is the string '```json\n{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}\n```\n{"c": 3}' and Reply B is the string '{"c": 3}'. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer to be the object {"b": 2}, because that is the last top-level balanced object inside the code fence. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer to be {"c": 3}, because there is no fence and it is the last top-level object. The function, however, returns {"c": 3} for both replies: for Reply A, the fenced pool contains the entire fence content '{"a": 1}\n{"b": 2}' as a single candidate, which fails to parse, so the function falls through to the top-level pool and returns the last top-level object, which is {"c": 3}. I would be wrong if the function actually returned {"b": 2} for Reply A, or if the documented rule were read as treating a fence's entire content as a single candidate that, when unparseable, is ignored entirely, in which case the rule would also require {"c": 3} for Reply A and the function would be correct.